<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Coverage settings for TechieBlog.Tests — REQ-NFR-016 (BRD-76).

  Run with dotnet test, passing the "XPlat Code Coverage" collector and pointing
  the settings switch at this file (see .github/workflows/ci.yml for the exact
  command line; MSBuild switches cannot be quoted inside an XML comment).

  ============================================================================
  DECLARED NARROWING OF THE COVERAGE SCOPE — owner decision, 2026-08-10
  ============================================================================
  READ THIS BEFORE QUOTING THE COVERAGE NUMBER ANYWHERE.

  The 80% line-coverage target in BRD-76 is measured against a DELIBERATELY
  NARROWED slice of BlogEngine, not against the whole assembly. This is a
  declared, documented reduction of scope. It is written down here, and again
  in the CI step that reads the report, so that nobody quotes the narrowed
  figure as if it were whole-assembly coverage. Anyone comparing this number to
  the pre-2026-08-10 figure is comparing two different things.

  WHAT IS EXCLUDED, AND WHY
  =========================
  Measured on 2026-08-09 and re-measured on 2026-08-10, BlogEngine had 9,455
  coverable lines and 47.2% line coverage. Three groups of code account for
  essentially all of the shortfall, and NONE of them can execute in a run that
  has no PostgreSQL server, no object store and no DI host:

    BlogEngine.DbAccess.*                      3,226 lines, measured 0.0%
        Every repository. Not one line runs without a live connection —
        the first statement of each method opens one. These are thin Dapper
        wrappers over SQL; a mock-based unit test would assert only that the
        mock was called, which is why none exists.

    BlogEngine.Storage.*                         237 lines, measured 0.0%
        FileSystemStorage / NetworkFileStorage / CloudFileStorage /
        LocalFileStorage and their factory. Need a real filesystem share or
        cloud endpoint, and FileStorageFactory additionally reads its provider
        choice from the settings table (so: needs the database too).

    BlogEngine.DaCore.DbConnectionFactory        + GenericRepository`1
                                                 ~103 lines, measured ~25%
        The connection factory and the repository base class every repository
        extends. Same reason as DbAccess: DB plumbing. NOTE the deliberate
        exception — BlogEngine.DaCore.DbTimestamp is a pure, connection-free
        helper and is KEPT IN SCOPE (it is at 100%). Only the two DB-bound
        types are excluded, not the whole DaCore namespace.

    BlogEngine.BlogSvcInitializer                 61 lines, measured 0.0%
    BlogEngine.EngagementSvcInitializer           21 lines, measured 0.0%
        DI service-registration entry points. They register types into an
        IServiceCollection; executing them proves nothing that a compile does
        not already prove, and they are exercised for real at host startup.

  Together those are ~3,566 of 9,455 lines that a no-database run can never
  reach, which puts the CEILING for the old whole-assembly scope at 62.3% —
  the 80% target was arithmetically unreachable as previously scoped.

  WHAT REMAINS IN SCOPE
  =====================
    BlogEngine.Services.*   — business logic, tested with NSubstitute fakes
    BlogEngine.Common.*     — pure helpers (slug, markdown, captcha, hashing…)
    BlogEngine.DaCore.DbTimestamp
    plus anything added to BlogEngine outside the excluded names above.

  The Include stays [BlogEngine]* on purpose. Scope is narrowed by an explicit
  EXCLUDE list, not by an allow-list, so that a namespace added to BlogEngine
  later is counted by default and shows up as a coverage drop rather than
  silently vanishing from the denominator.

  WHAT WOULD HAVE TO CHANGE TO WIDEN IT AGAIN
  ===========================================
  Delete the exclusion for a group once its code can actually run in CI:

    * DbAccess + DaCore DB plumbing — requires the opt-in Testcontainers suite
      (TechieBlogIntegrationTests=true, tests/TechieBlog.Tests/Integration/) to
      run in the same leg that collects coverage. It is OFF today: CI has no
      Docker-in-Docker step, and the standing project rule forbids standing up
      a second PostgreSQL container locally. Turning it on would also make the
      coverage leg depend on a container pull, so it is a CI-cost decision, not
      just a settings edit.

    * Storage — needs either a temp-directory fixture for FileSystemStorage /
      NetworkFileStorage (cheap, no infrastructure, the obvious next win) or a
      cloud emulator for CloudFileStorage (not cheap).

    * The two SvcInitializers — need a host-builder smoke test that resolves
      the registered services from a built ServiceProvider.

  Each removal will LOWER the reported percentage until tests for that group
  land. That is the intended behaviour: the number must move when the scope
  moves.
  ============================================================================
-->
<RunSettings>
  <DataCollectionRunSettings>
    <DataCollectors>
      <DataCollector friendlyName="XPlat code coverage">
        <Configuration>
          <Format>cobertura,opencover</Format>

          <!-- BlogEngine is the coverage target; every other assembly is out. -->
          <Include>[BlogEngine]*</Include>

          <!--
            Other assemblies, then the declared narrowing documented above.
            Do not add an entry here without adding its justification to that
            comment block — an undocumented exclusion is a silently inflated
            number, which is exactly what this file exists to prevent.
          -->
          <!-- Kept on ONE line: coverlet splits this value on ',' without
               trimming, so a wrapped list yields filters with leading
               whitespace that silently match nothing. The readable, annotated
               form of this list is the comment block above. -->
          <Exclude>[BlogModels]*,[BlogUI]*,[TechieBlog]*,[TechieBlog.Tests]*,[BlogDb]*,[BlogEngine]BlogEngine.DbAccess.*,[BlogEngine]BlogEngine.Storage.*,[BlogEngine]BlogEngine.DaCore.DbConnectionFactory*,[BlogEngine]BlogEngine.DaCore.GenericRepository*,[BlogEngine]BlogEngine.BlogSvcInitializer,[BlogEngine]BlogEngine.EngagementSvcInitializer</Exclude>

          <ExcludeByAttribute>Obsolete,GeneratedCodeAttribute,CompilerGeneratedAttribute</ExcludeByAttribute>
          <SingleHit>false</SingleHit>
          <UseSourceLink>true</UseSourceLink>
          <IncludeTestAssembly>false</IncludeTestAssembly>
          <SkipAutoProps>true</SkipAutoProps>
        </Configuration>
      </DataCollector>
    </DataCollectors>
  </DataCollectionRunSettings>
</RunSettings>
